            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to relax, then the Latin for relaxation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ālea, āleae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ālea, āleae — \"dice-playing, a game of chance\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mīlitēs in castrīs āleā lūdunt. The soldiers play dice in the camp."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, first declension. Gambling at dice was forbidden except at festival time. Ālea iacta est — the die is cast."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ālea is a word of uncertain origin; Lewis & Short reports a guess linking it with a word for bone, but nothing is settled. English aleatory, depending on chance, comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
